findings.body:
The `From<tray_icon::MouseButtonState>` impl used to map `Up` to `Up` and `Down` to `Down`. This PR swaps them, so every tray click event now reports the opposite button state to JavaScript listeners. The derive refactor was supposed to be mechanical, and this edit is not part of it.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="31-38"}

The existing test `tray_event_json_serialization` builds the enum directly and never goes through the `From` impl, so it does not catch the swap. Any frontend code that reacts to `buttonState === "Down"` will now see the wrong value for every click.

::callout{type="warning"}
Revert these two match arms to the original identity mapping. Consider adding a unit test that converts each `tray_icon::MouseButtonState` variant and asserts the matching `MouseButtonState`.

Before this PR, `#[cfg(feature = "isolation")]` applied only to the `impl Default for IsolationSide` block. That block was removed, but the attribute was left in place. It now sits directly above `#[derive(Template)]`, so it gates the `PatternJavascript` struct.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/pattern.rs" lines="65-83"}

`PatternJavascript` is constructed in `crates/tauri/src/manager/webview.rs` around line 131 with no cfg guard around it. A build without the `isolation` feature should fail with an unresolved-name error. Only isolation builds would compile.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/pattern.rs" lines="78-83"}
#[derive(Template)]
#[default_template("../scripts/pattern.js")]
pub(crate) struct PatternJavascript {
  pub(crate) pattern: PatternObject,
}

Remove the `#[cfg(feature = "isolation")]` line that sits above `#[derive(Template)]`, so the struct is available in every build as it was before.

This PR adds `TrayIconEvent::new_click` as a `pub` method. The method is unrelated to the clippy fix. It has no call sites in the repository, and the only in-repo test builds the enum literal directly. It also puts test scaffolding ("Useful for testing or creating mock events") into the public API of a shipped crate, and that becomes a permanent surface to maintain.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="137-151"}

If a test helper is needed, make it `pub(crate)` and put it inside the `#[cfg(test)]` module. Otherwise, drop it from this PR and reintroduce it in a separate change that explains the public use case.

The doc comment on `Up` says "Mouse button pressed." and the comment on `Down` says "Mouse button released." That is backwards. This was already present before the PR, but the PR touches this exact enum and adds `#[default]` to `Up`, so it is a good time to fix it. The default value is the release state, so the comment on the default variant is misleading to anyone reading the derive.

::code{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="21-29"}

Swap the two doc comments so that `Up` is documented as released and `Down` as pressed.

Across tauri-utils (config and config_v1), tauri-runtime, tauri-cli, tauri-bundler, and tauri, the hand-written `impl Default` blocks are replaced with `#[derive(Default)]` plus `#[default]` on the same variant that the old impl returned. I checked each pair against the removed bodies and the chosen variants match. The `#[default]` attribute is stable on enum variants, so the MSRV is not affected.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-utils/src/config.rs" lines="801-835"}

The bundler `error.rs` change is also equivalent. The two `#[cfg(target_os)]` variants are collapsed into one variant with `cfg_attr`-selected messages, and the variant name and message text are preserved on each platform.
